# Coding Standards

Write code that is concise, defensive, and performant. Optimization is a first-class priority: every change must consider CPU time, memory use, stack use, binary size, latency, and I/O cost.

## Repository Context

- This is an ESP32 smart speaker firmware/backend repository. Firmware code is built with ESP-IDF/ESP-ADF and includes Bluetooth audio, Spotify Connect, audio pipelines, Wi-Fi, WebSocket voice assistant flows, and embedded audio hardware integration.
- Prefer simple, deterministic designs suitable for constrained embedded systems.
- Keep abstractions thin. Avoid framework-style indirection unless it removes measurable duplication or risk.

## C++ / C Best Practices

- Prefer modern C++ with RAII, clear ownership, `const` correctness, narrow scopes, and explicit lifetimes.
- Avoid raw owning pointers. Use references, values, `std::unique_ptr`, or existing ESP-IDF ownership patterns as appropriate.
- Avoid exceptions and RTTI in firmware paths unless the project already enables and uses them safely.
- Avoid dynamic allocation in hot paths, ISR-adjacent paths, audio callbacks, and frequently executed loops.
- Use fixed-size buffers when bounds are known. Validate every length before copying, parsing, or indexing.
- Prefer `std::array`, `std::span`, `string_view`, and explicit buffer sizes over unchecked pointer arithmetic when available.
- Do not introduce global mutable state unless it models hardware/system state and is protected by clear ownership or synchronization.

## Performance Rules

- Treat audio, networking, and state-transition paths as latency-sensitive.
- Minimize allocations, copies, lock contention, logging, and blocking calls in runtime paths.
- Prefer zero-copy or bounded-copy flows for audio buffers and protocol frames.
- Avoid unnecessary heap fragmentation. Reuse buffers deliberately and release resources predictably.
- Keep stack usage conservative. Large buffers must be static, heap-backed with explicit ownership, or externally provided.
- Do not add polling loops, sleeps, or retries without bounded timing and failure behavior.

## Defensive Coding

- Check all return values from ESP-IDF, FreeRTOS, socket/WebSocket, filesystem, NVS, audio, and C library calls.
- Fail closed with explicit error handling. Never silently ignore initialization, allocation, parse, or I/O failures.
- Validate external input from Bluetooth, Spotify, WebSocket, backend responses, configuration, NVS, and audio streams.
- Make state machines explicit. Guard invalid transitions and log actionable errors without flooding logs.
- Prefer early returns for invalid preconditions and cleanup paths that are easy to audit.

## Memory Management

- Memory management must be conservative, precise, and reviewable.
- Document ownership transfer at API boundaries.
- Pair every allocation/acquire with a clear release path, preferably RAII.
- Avoid ambiguous borrowed pointers. Make borrowed lifetimes obvious in names, types, or comments.
- Clear or reset sensitive credentials/tokens when no longer needed.
- Do not retain pointers to transient buffers, stack memory, event payloads, or temporary strings.

## Concurrency and Embedded Constraints

- Keep task ownership, queue ownership, and callback threading assumptions explicit.
- Do not block audio callbacks, event handlers, Wi-Fi callbacks, or high-priority tasks.
- Protect shared state with the smallest practical synchronization scope.
- Prefer message passing or queues over shared mutable state when crossing task boundaries.
- Keep ISR-safe code minimal and only call APIs that are safe in that context.

## Style and Change Discipline

- Make the smallest correct change. Avoid broad refactors mixed with functional fixes.
- Match existing naming, formatting, logging style, and ESP-IDF idioms.
- Keep functions short and single-purpose. Remove dead code instead of commenting it out.
- Add comments only where they clarify non-obvious constraints, hardware assumptions, timing, ownership, or performance tradeoffs.
- Do not add dependencies unless they are essential, lightweight, and justified.
- Do not change SDK config, partition layout, build flags, or hardware pins unless the task explicitly requires it.

## Validation

- Prefer compile-time checks, static assertions, bounds checks, and deterministic tests where practical.
- Before finishing, consider whether the change affects boot, Wi-Fi, Bluetooth, Spotify auth, audio routing, microphone streaming, WebSocket flows, NVS persistence, or memory pressure.
- When build/test commands are available, run the narrowest relevant command first, then the broader build if needed.
